## Input files msil-ld cannot link (ImplementationPlan.md 5.9.4; 5.9.14;
## 5.10.5 for ILAsm text given to a link; 5.10.8 and 5.10.14 for native
## libraries from M15):
## - a file that does not exist: lld's 'cannot open <file>: <reason>';
## - a file of no known kind (text, ILAsm text, an empty file): 'unknown file
##   type: <file>';
## - a PE with a CLI header (5.9.4): from M20 a CIL object links (obj-*.test),
##   a .NET assembly (an Assembly row, here an EXE msil-ld wrote) is
##   '<file>: .NET assembly inputs are not supported yet' (M21 binds
##   reference assemblies, M22 links libraries), and a module without the
##   link-info record (a netmodule ilasm writes from text without
##   '.assembly') is '<file>: not a CIL object (no link-info record)'; M14's
##   one error for CIL objects and assemblies is gone. make-pe.py's minimal
##   CLI image (a metadata root without streams) is no object either: the
##   metadata reader's error, with 5.7.12's prefix 'invalid CLI metadata: ',
##   names the file and the missing '#~' stream (obj-input-errors.test
##   covers the record and object errors);
## - a native PE is a native library: M15 reads its export table (this one
##   has none, so it binds nothing and changes nothing);
## - a native library that does not parse is an error naming the file (a
##   shared object and an import library cut short, which still have their
##   magic), and so is one for another machine (5.10.14), never a crash;
## - a shared object whose DT_SONAME lies outside its string table is
##   '<file>: invalid DT_SONAME entry', and an import library whose short
##   import member has a SizeOfData past the member or an unknown name type
##   is '<lib>(<member>): broken import library' (lld-link's text);
## - a DLL whose export directory gives a count that runs its table past the
##   image (NumberOfNamePointers 0x80000001, AddressTableEntries 0x40000001,
##   which overflow a 32-bit size) is '<file>: malformed export table' (the
##   section 12 row of 2026-10-22 for 5.10.8), never a misleading error, a
##   binding read from unvalidated bytes or a crash;
## - an archive member that is not LLVM bitcode is the warning
##   '<archive>(<member>): not LLVM bitcode; ignored', and the link goes on;
## - bitcode with module-level inline assembly, positional or an archive
##   member, with --whole-archive or lazy (msil-ld reads no archive index
##   and reads every member), is '<file>: module-level inline assembly is
##   not supported for MSIL' (D26's text) with exit code 1, never
##   ModuleSymbolTable's assertion or a stack dump (5.9.14, section 12 row
##   '5.9.14 gains a diagnostic (review-r2 finding 5)' and its amendment for
##   lazy members). No MSIL triple has an asm parser, so the bitcode writer
##   stores no symbol table for such a module, and LTO's InputFile would
##   rebuild it and assert. Its archive is written without an index ('S')
##   and in GNU format: the tree's llvm-ar asserts on this bitcode when it
##   builds an index, or when it picks the COFF format a windows-msvc member
##   suggests (an upstream-generic limit of targets without an asm parser
##   that M14 leaves alone).
## The native PE inputs are written by Inputs/make-pe.py; only the netmodule
## needs ilasm.

